WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:01.870
You gave the right answer.

2
00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.880
And you still got no offer.

3
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:04.355
Why?

4
00:00:04.375 --> 00:00:07.615
The interviewer could not write down proof that you are senior.

5
00:00:07.635 --> 00:00:09.940
This track fixes that with three habits:

6
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.665
show signal, talk out loud, and use numbers.

7
00:00:13.685 --> 00:00:16.100
First: how is a senior loop scored?

8
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.238
Each interviewer writes a short report.

9
00:00:18.258 --> 00:00:21.310
A group reads those reports and decides.

10
00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.573
So a round with no clear evidence counts as a weak round.

11
00:00:24.593 --> 00:00:26.760
Even if you were right.

12
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:29.470
Second: what does senior signal sound like?

13
00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.983
A mid-level answer solves the task.

14
00:00:32.003 --> 00:00:35.055
A senior answer also explains the trade-off.

15
00:00:35.075 --> 00:00:36.542
Ask about scale first.

16
00:00:36.562 --> 00:00:38.080
Name the failure cases.

17
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.505
And say what you would monitor.

18
00:00:40.525 --> 00:00:43.040
Third: run every round out loud.

19
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:44.377
Clarify the question.

20
00:00:44.397 --> 00:00:45.252
State your plan.

21
00:00:45.272 --> 00:00:45.990
Build it.

22
00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.175
Then test it with an edge case.

23
00:00:48.195 --> 00:00:50.563
Silence gives the interviewer nothing to write.

24
00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:52.800
So think in words.

25
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.235
Last: know a few numbers by heart.

26
00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:58.822
One day has about 86,400 seconds.

27
00:00:58.842 --> 00:01:01.870
Round it to 100,000 for quick maths.

28
00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:06.820
With that, you can turn events per day into events per second, in your head.

29
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.830
Quick recap of the playbook.

30
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.318
Leave written evidence.

31
00:01:10.338 --> 00:01:11.655
Explain the trade-off.

32
00:01:11.675 --> 00:01:14.290
And run the round out loud, with numbers.

33
00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.410
Now start the first lesson below.
